Fix a reference-counting bug in the waveform renderer

PyArg_ParseTuple() with the "O!" format returns a borrowed reference, so the
two Py_DECREF (samples) calls (the error path and the success path) drop a
reference the function never owned. AudioPreviewer._render_waveform() passes a
freshly sliced list, whose refcount then drops to zero while the caller still
uses it: the list is freed twice, the heap is corrupted, and with Python 3.14
the corruption surfaces as a segfault in the next garbage collection.

This is a local patch, not an upstream change; the two Py_DECREF calls are
simply removed because the argument is owned by the caller and lives until
fill_surface() returns.

--- a/pitivi/coptimizations/renderer.c
+++ b/pitivi/coptimizations/renderer.c
@@ -53,7 +53,7 @@
     /* If the object was not a float or convertible to float */
     if (PyErr_Occurred ()) {
       cairo_surface_finish (surface);
-      Py_DECREF (samples);
+      /* borrowed reference; do not decref */
       return NULL;
     }
 
@@ -70,7 +70,7 @@
     x += pixelsPerSample;
   }
 
-  Py_DECREF (samples);
+  /* borrowed from PyArg_ParseTuple; do not decref */
   cairo_line_to (ctx, width, height);
   cairo_close_path (ctx);
   cairo_fill_preserve (ctx);
